# Filename must still say what the file does

When you make a **substantial** change to a file — new responsibility, new public surface, or the file now primarily does something else — stop and ask whether the current name still describes that job. If it does not, **suggest a rename** and wait for a yes. Do not rename unasked.

A substantial change is a shift in what the file *is for*, not a bugfix, a helper, or an extra case inside the same job.

```text
# BAD — extractor.py now also resolves calendar dates and you ship it under
# the old name with no comment
# (filename no longer matches)

# GOOD — after the change, say so and wait
# "extractor.py now owns date resolution as well as extraction.
# Rename to date_resolve.py (or similar)? "
```

Do not suggest a rename for a one-line fix, a new private helper, or extra coverage of the same responsibility. If several files moved together, name the set, not one file in isolation.
